<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1982</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as UNCLOS</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) is an international treaty adopted in 1982 after nine years of negotiation and in force since 1994. It defines a 12-nautical-mile territorial sea, a 200-mile exclusive economic zone and rights over the continental shelf, and recognises archipelagic states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. It also created the International Tribunal for the Law of the Sea and the International Seabed Authority.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-nations-convention-on-the-law-of-the-sea.jpg" alt="United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UNCLOS_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
